peed":[{"time":22.8,"value":1,"ease":"smooth"},{"time":23.8,"value":0.55,"ease":"smooth"},{"time":28,"value":0.45,"ease":"smooth"}],"visual.letterbox":[{"time":23.4,"value":0,"ease":"smooth"},{"time":25,"value":1,"ease":"smooth"},{"time":28,"value":1,"ease":"smooth"}]},"autoKey":false},"hook":{"text":"Keep the same compass heading forever…"},"youtube":{"title":"The loxodrome: steer one heading and spiral into the pole 🧭 #shorts","description":"On a unit globe: (cos λ / cosh kλ, tanh kλ, sin λ / cosh kλ) with k = 0.15, so the latitude is the Gudermannian gd(kλ) of the longitude.\n\nWhich one should I make next? 👇\n\n#math #science #shorts","tags":"math, science, chaos, math art, math animation, physics, satisfying, shorts"},"about":{"title":"Loxodrome","sources":[{"label":"Rhumb line (loxodrome)","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rhumb_line"}],"math":"On a unit globe: (cos λ / cosh kλ, tanh kλ, sin λ / cosh kλ) with k = 0.15, so the latitude is the Gudermannian gd(kλ) of the longitude.","aha":"Sailing a fixed compass bearing crosses every meridian at the same angle, so you circle the pole infinitely often yet arrive after a finite distance. On a Mercator map this spiral is a straight line, which is why sailors loved Mercator’s map."}}]}
